    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A French barn door is a glass-paned door hung on barn-door hardware. It solves one problem very well: getting daylight into a room without giving up floor space to a door swing. It does not seal, it does not give you much privacy unless you choose the glass carefully, and it is far heavier than it looks. Get those three things right and it is one of the best-looking ways to close off an opening.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a French Barn Door Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            The name mixes two door types, so it is worth being precise. A French door, in the traditional sense, is a pair of hinged doors with glass panes divided by muntins that swing open from the centre and close against a frame. A barn door is a slab that hangs from rollers on an exposed overhead track and slides across the face of the wall. A French barn door takes the look of the first and the mechanism of the second: a glass-paned panel, usually with a grid of divided lites, hanging from barn-door rollers and sliding sideways instead of swinging.
          </p>

          <p>
            That distinction matters because the two are often confused in search results and in showrooms. If you are deciding between hinged French doors and a sliding barn door, that is a different question with different answers, and our <a href="/barn-door-vs-french-door/">barn door vs French door comparison</a> covers it. This page is about the hybrid: what happens when you put French-door glass on a barn-door track, and what that changes about privacy, weight, sizing and cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also not the same thing as the sliding French doors sold for patios. Those are exterior units that slide within a sealed frame, with weatherstripping, a sill and a locking mechanism, and they are designed to keep weather out. A French barn door is an interior door that hangs in front of an opening rather than inside it. Using one on an exterior wall, or as a substitute for a sealed patio door, is not what the format is built for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within the category there is a lot of variety. Glass layouts range from a single full-height pane to grids of ten or fifteen small lites, and some doors split the difference with a solid lower panel and glass above. Frames are made from solid wood, painted MDF, aluminium or steel. Divided lites can be true divided, where each small pane is a separate piece of glass, or simulated, where one large pane carries an applied grid. The simulated version is generally lighter, cheaper and easier to clean, and on a sliding door, where weight is the main engineering constraint, that is a real advantage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where French Barn Doors Work, and Where They Don't
          </h2>

          <p>
            The strongest case for a glass barn door is an interior room with little or no natural light of its own. A home office carved out of a hallway, a pantry off a kitchen, a den in the middle of a floor plan: a solid door turns those spaces into caves, and a hinged door eats into the floor area of rooms that are usually small. Glass on a sliding track solves both problems at once, which is why it has become a common choice for exactly these openings.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second good fit is a wide opening between two living spaces that you mostly want open. A pair of glass barn doors between a living room and a dining room or den keeps the sightline and the light when closed, slides fully clear of the opening when open, and avoids two door leaves swinging into the room. In open-plan homes it gives you the option of a separate room without making the space feel walled off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it works badly is anywhere that needs genuine acoustic or visual privacy. A main bathroom shared by a household, a bedroom off a busy living area, a home office used for frequent calls: these all need a door that closes into a frame and latches, and a barn door of any kind does neither. Glass makes the privacy question harder, not easier. The <a href="/best-barn-doors/">barn door buying guide</a> is blunt about the format's limits, and every one of them applies here.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Opening</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Good fit?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Home office (quiet, solo work)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Borrows light, saves floor space; visual separation is enough</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Home office (frequent calls)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gaps pass sound; glass offers little privacy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pantry or laundry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No privacy needs; glass keeps a small room bright</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Living to dining room (pair)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keeps sightlines; opens fully clear of the opening</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ensuite off a bedroom</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Maybe</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only with obscure glass, and accept the gaps</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shared main bathroom</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs a latching door that seals to a frame</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="privacy-light" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Privacy and Light Trade-Off
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every glass door involves a trade between the light it passes and the privacy it gives up, and the glass type is where you set that balance. Clear glass passes the most light and gives no privacy at all. It suits a dining room or a den where the point is to keep the rooms visually connected. Frosted or acid-etched glass diffuses light evenly and blurs everything behind it to shapes and colours. Reeded or fluted glass, with its vertical ribs, distorts the view while still letting a good deal of light through, and it has become a popular middle ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            One effect catches people out: obscure glass is not equally private in both directions at all times. During the day, with the brighter side outside the room, frosted glass hides the room well. At night, with the lights on inside and the hallway dark, shapes and movement inside become much more visible from the dark side. If the door is on a room used in the evening, look at sample glass with a light behind it before ordering, not just in a showroom under even lighting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Divided lites change the balance too. A grid of muntins breaks up the view and reads as more private than a single sheet of the same glass, but the effect is mostly visual. A fifteen-lite door with clear glass still shows everything behind it. If privacy matters, the glass type does the work and the grid is decoration.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a clear-glass door, applied privacy film is the usual retrofit. It can be removed later, it is cheap compared with replacing glass, and it gives you a choice of frosted, reeded-effect or gradient finishes. The trade-off is durability and appearance up close, since film can lift at the edges of small panes and shows seams where a grid is cut around. Curtains or blinds fixed to the door are another option, but anything mounted to the door adds weight and has to clear the wall as the door slides.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Choose the glass for the room's evening use, not its daytime look.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Frosted and reeded glass hide a room well when it is dark inside and bright outside. With the lights on inside at night, the effect partly reverses. Check samples with a light behind them before you commit.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Remember that the glass is only part of the privacy picture. A barn door hangs in front of the opening with a gap at the sides and the bottom, so light spills around the edges whatever the glass. On a room where you want darkness, such as a bedroom, those glowing edges are often more annoying than the glass itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing Single and Double French Barn Doors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sizing follows the same rules as any barn door, with a few extra considerations for pairs. A single door should be two to three inches wider than the opening on each side and two to three inches taller at the top, so that it overlaps the casing rather than sitting inside it. For a standard 32 inch interior doorway that means a slab of roughly 36 to 38 inches. The overlap is what covers the opening and limits the light and sound that pass around the edges. Glass doors are usually made to standard sizes rather than to order, so this is a matter of choosing the nearest stock width that overlaps properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a pair meeting in the middle, add the overlap to both sides of the opening and then split the total between two doors. A 60 inch opening with a two-inch overlap each side needs 64 inches of coverage, so two doors of about 32 inches each. The track then has to be long enough for each door to park completely clear of the opening, which works out at roughly twice the total coverage. In that example the track run is in the region of 128 inches, or nearly eleven feet, which is longer than many single kits and is why double-door kits are sold separately or with joiners.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Opening width</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coverage with 2 in overlap</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Each door</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. track run</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">48 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">104 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">64 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">32 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">128 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">72 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">76 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">38 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">152 in</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those figures are arithmetic from the overlap rule, not a substitute for the kit instructions, and stock door widths will not always match exactly. The point is the wall space they imply. A pair needs a clear stretch of wall on both sides of the opening, each at least as wide as one door. That is often the deciding factor in a living room, where one side of a wide opening frequently has a light switch, an outlet or a return wall. If only one side is clear, a single wide door or a bypass kit, where two doors run on parallel tracks and stack behind each other, may be the only workable layout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Height needs checking as a stack. Door height plus roller hardware plus track has to fit between the top of the casing and the ceiling, and glass doors are often sold in standard heights, so there is less room to trim. Wood-framed doors can sometimes be cut down slightly at the bottom rail, but glass panels cannot, and cutting into a frame can void a warranty. Measure the clearance first.
          </p>

          <p>
            With a pair, the two doors also need to hang at exactly the same height and meet cleanly in the middle. That means a level track along its full length, careful adjustment of the hangers, and guides that keep both doors close to the wall. Doors that meet in the middle cannot use a single floor guide at the centre of the opening, so pairs rely on guides at the outer ends of travel or on wall-mounted guides. Some kits add stops or a centre latch to keep the doors aligned when closed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-hardware" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Glass Weight and Hardware Ratings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Glass is heavy, and that is the single most important fact about a French barn door. Glass suppliers such as Bendheim put quarter-inch glass at roughly three pounds per square foot. A 36 by 84 inch door is 21 square feet, so if most of that area is quarter-inch glass, the glass alone could be in the region of 50 to 60 pounds before you add the frame. A solid wood or steel frame adds considerably more. Check the maker's listed weight for the specific door before choosing hardware.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hardware ratings need reading with the same scepticism as for any barn door. Consumer kits are typically rated between 200 and 400 pounds, and that figure is a static rating. A moving door produces dynamic loads above its own weight, especially when it hits the end stop. Choose hardware rated comfortably above the door's actual weight, not at it. For a pair, check whether the rating is per door or for the whole track, because kits describe this differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soft-close dampers matter more on glass than on wood. A heavy door gathers momentum, and glass arriving at an end stop with a bang is both loud and hard on the panes, particularly in a divided-lite door where small panes sit in glazing beads. Dampers that catch the door in the last few inches are a sensible upgrade on any heavy glass door, and our <a href="/best-barn-doors/">best barn doors guide</a> covers a dual soft-close kit rated for heavy slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mounting has to carry all of that weight into the structure. Drywall anchors are not adequate for any barn door and are especially wrong for a glass one. Every fixing needs to land in a stud, or the track needs to mount on a solid backer board fixed across several studs. A <a href="/best-stud-finder/">good stud finder</a> is worth having for this job, and if you do not own one, <a href="/how-to-find-a-stud-without-a-stud-finder/">there are reliable ways to find studs without one</a>. Do not assume the framing continues along the whole track run just because there is a header above the opening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Safety glazing is not optional. The International Residential Code treats glazing in fixed and operable door panels, including sliding doors, as a hazardous location that requires safety glazing, with narrow exceptions for very small openings and decorative glass. In practice that means tempered or laminated glass, and safety glazing is normally identified by a permanent mark etched or fused into a corner of each pane. Buy from a maker that states the glass is tempered, check for the mark, and confirm with your local building department, since codes are adopted and amended locally.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sound-gaps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sound, Gaps and What Glass Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Glass does not fix the barn door's main weakness. A barn door hangs in front of the opening rather than closing into a frame, so there is a gap all the way round: typically about half an inch at the sides, and a gap at the bottom so the door clears the floor. Sound, light and smells pass through those gaps whatever the door is made of. Glass is a dense material, but the gaps dominate how much sound gets through, so a glass barn door is not meaningfully quieter than a solid one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Glass can also add a noise of its own. In a divided-lite door, loose glazing beads or panes can rattle when the door moves or hits its stop. That is a build quality issue, and it is worth checking reviews and owner photos for any mention of it before buying. Soft-close dampers reduce the problem by removing the impact at the end of travel.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can narrow the gaps, but not eliminate them. A generous overlap on the sides and top reduces the direct line through the edge gap. Mounting the track so the door hangs as close to the wall as the hardware allows helps too, as does keeping the floor clearance to the minimum your flooring allows, typically around half an inch. A rug on the far side softens sound reflected in the room. None of these turns a barn door into a sealed door.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need a room that is genuinely quiet or genuinely private, a door that closes into a jamb is the answer. A hinged French door with weatherstripping is one option. A pocket door is another, since it slides into the wall and closes against a jamb on the latch side, and it can take a proper privacy latch. Our <a href="/pocket-door-vs-barn-door/">pocket door vs barn door comparison</a> covers that trade, including what it costs to open a wall to fit one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What French Barn Doors Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prices vary widely, so it is more useful to look at published ranges than any single figure. Fixr's barn door cost guide, last updated in January 2024, lists glass barn doors at roughly $246 to $2,470 for the door alone, depending on material and size. Its standard hardware kits run from about $42 to $447 depending on track quality and length, and custom track and hardware can cost $600 to $1,200 or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labour is the other half. The same guide puts professional installation of a barn door with hardware at roughly $308 to $984, depending on track size and location. It describes a mid-range barn door project, which can include one or two glass doors, at $600 to $2,500, and a luxury installation with tempered glass or hardwood at $1,200 to $5,000 per door. Treat these as national ranges from one source rather than quotes; local labour rates and the condition of your wall change the picture.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Published Cost Ranges (Fixr, Jan 2024)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$246-$2,470</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Glass barn door, door only</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$42-$447</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Standard hardware kit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$308-$984</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pro install with hardware</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$600-$2,500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mid-range project</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A pair roughly doubles the door cost and needs a longer track or a double-door kit, so budget accordingly. Fixr notes that double barn doors cost more than singles but does not give a separate figure, so price your specific doors and hardware rather than relying on a multiplier. The hidden extras are a backer board, better fixings than the ones supplied, soft-close dampers, and moving a switch or outlet that sits in the door's path, which needs an electrician.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where to spend is the same as for any barn door: on the hardware first. A modest glass door on smooth, properly rated rollers with soft-close will feel more expensive than a beautiful door on a cheap track. And measure the wall space, headroom and framing before buying anything, because those decide whether this is a weekend job or a project that involves opening up a wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing hardware rated at exactly the door's weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Glass doors are heavy, and kit ratings are static. A moving door produces dynamic loads above its own weight, especially at the end stop. Pick a track rated comfortably above the door's listed weight, and for a pair check whether the rating is per door or per track.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying clear glass for a room that needs privacy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear glass on a home office, ensuite or bedroom shows everything behind it, and a grid of muntins does not change that. Pick frosted, reeded or other obscure glass for those rooms, and check samples with a light behind them for night-time use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting a pair needs wall space on both sides</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Double barn doors with glass each park beside the opening, so both sides need a clear stretch of wall at least one door wide. A switch, outlet or return wall on either side can rule out a pair, so walk both walls before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting the track into drywall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A glass door and its frame can easily weigh more than a solid wood slab. Drywall anchors will fail, often suddenly. Land every fixing in a stud or mount a backer board across several studs and fix the track to that.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the safety glazing check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Building codes treat glass in sliding and swinging doors as a hazardous location. Confirm the door uses tempered or laminated glass and look for the permanent safety mark on each pane, especially on imported or secondhand doors.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a French barn door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A French barn door is a glass-paned door, usually with a grid of divided lites like a traditional French door, that hangs from an exposed overhead track and slides sideways like a barn door. It borrows light between rooms without a swing arc, but it keeps the barn door's gaps around the edges, so it does not seal or latch like a hinged French door.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are glass barn doors private?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you choose the glass for it. Clear glass offers no privacy. Frosted, acid-etched and reeded glass blur the view well in daylight, but with lights on inside at night, shapes become more visible from a dark hallway. Light also leaks around the edges of any barn door. For bathrooms used by a whole household, a latching hinged door is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How heavy is a French barn door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the glass and frame, so check the listed weight of the specific door. As a guide, glass suppliers put quarter-inch glass at about three pounds per square foot, so a 36 by 84 inch door with mostly glass could carry 50 to 60 pounds of glass before the frame is added. Choose hardware rated comfortably above the total.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size barn doors do I need for a double opening?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add two to three inches of overlap on each side of the opening, then split the total between two doors. A 60 inch opening needs about 64 inches of coverage, or two doors of roughly 32 inches. The track needs to be about twice the total coverage so both doors can clear the opening, and each side needs clear wall at least one door wide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do glass barn doors need tempered glass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes in practice. The International Residential Code treats glazing in door panels, including sliding doors, as a hazardous location that requires safety glazing, with narrow exceptions for very small openings and decorative glass. That means tempered or laminated glass, normally marked permanently on each pane. Your local building department has the final word because codes are adopted locally.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are sliding French doors the same as French barn doors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Sliding French doors usually means an exterior patio unit that slides inside a sealed frame with weatherstripping, a sill and a lock. A French barn door is an interior door that hangs in front of an opening on exposed hardware and does not seal. Do not use a barn-door style unit where you need a weather-tight exterior door.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a French barn door cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fixr's barn door cost guide, updated January 2024, lists glass barn doors at about $246 to $2,470 for the door alone, standard hardware kits at about $42 to $447, and professional installation at about $308 to $984. A pair costs more because you buy two doors and a longer track. Get local quotes, since labour rates vary.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

